<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  探索 is a real browser (spec 053), and a browser must be able to open an
	  http page: refusing would break a redirect chain that dips through http
	  and would make the local test harness unreachable.

	  It does NOT weaken the wallet. This key covers WEB CONTENT only — the
	  app's own requests still require TLS (`CoreHTTP.request` refuses anything
	  that is not https, and will keep refusing) — and `dapp_permissions`
	  refuses to sign for an insecure origin on its own, as `insecure_origin`.
	  So an http page can be READ and cannot ask for a signature.

	  `NSAllowsLocalNetworking` is for the device harness: the UI-test runner
	  serves the test dApp on 127.0.0.1:8137. `file://` is not an option, because
	  `dapp_origin_of` gives a file URL no origin at all — every signature would
	  fail closed and the harness would prove nothing.

	  Spec 088: the wallet itself is offered only to a SECURE CONTEXT — https, or
	  http on this device's loopback (`dapp_permissions::is_secure_context`, and
	  `window.isSecureContext` in the injected script). An http page on the
	  internet or on a LAN loads, with no provider in it. Spec 091: with
	  Settings' hidden debug mode on, http pages on this device's own network
	  are offered the wallet too (`dapp_permissions::offers_wallet`).
	-->
	<!--
	  Local network (spec 088, audit I20). The app opens no socket of its own on
	  the LAN and browses for nothing (no Bonjour), but a person can point it
	  there: a page typed into 探索, or a self-hosted service address (RPC,
	  index, relay) on their own network. Either one raises iOS's local-network
	  prompt, and this is the sentence it shows.
	-->
	<key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Vela reaches your local network only to open a page or a service address on it that you entered yourself.</string>
	<!--
	  The build's commit, for 关于 (spec 058). Android reads
	  `BuildConfig.GIT_COMMIT`, which its Gradle file fills by running git at
	  configuration time. iOS cannot: `ENABLE_USER_SCRIPT_SANDBOXING = YES`
	  means a build phase may not read `.git`, and a phase that quietly failed
	  would stamp a plausible wrong hash.

	  So it is a build SETTING, defaulting to `unknown`, and a release passes
	  the real one:

	      xcodebuild archive … VELA_GIT_COMMIT=$(git rev-parse --short HEAD)

	  When it is `unknown` the About page says `build <CFBundleVersion>`
	  instead — true, and never a hash nobody can look up.
	-->
	<key>VelaGitCommit</key>
	<string>$(VELA_GIT_COMMIT)</string>
	<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
	<dict>
		<key>NSAllowsArbitraryLoadsInWebContent</key>
		<true/>
		<key>NSAllowsLocalNetworking</key>
		<true/>
	</dict>
	<key>UIAppFonts</key>
	<array>
		<string>PlusJakartaSans_400Regular.ttf</string>
		<string>PlusJakartaSans_500Medium.ttf</string>
		<string>PlusJakartaSans_600SemiBold.ttf</string>
		<string>PlusJakartaSans_700Bold.ttf</string>
	</array>
	<!--
	  保存图片 on the receive screen (spec 051): the card is rendered in the app
	  and written to the album, and nothing is ever read back. `addOnly` is the
	  narrowest authorisation Photos offers, and a wallet asking to READ
	  somebody's photos in order to save a QR code would be asking for far more
	  than it needs.
	-->
	<!--
	  The scanner (spec 055): reading a payment code, a receive code, or a
	  WalletConnect-style URI with the camera. The system shows this sentence
	  the first time the viewfinder is opened, and never again — so it says what
	  the camera is FOR rather than that a camera exists.
	-->
	<!--
	  `velawallet://` (spec 056). Two shapes reach the app: `pay` prefills a
	  send, `open` hands a URL to the in-app browser. Universal links to
	  `getvela.app` ride the `applinks:` entitlement, which has been there since
	  019 with nothing behind it.
	-->
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleURLName</key>
			<string>app.getvela.VelaWallet</string>
			<key>CFBundleTypeRole</key>
			<string>Editor</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>velawallet</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<!--
	  Export compliance (spec 088, audit I7). The encryption in this app:

	    - HTTPS/TLS, through the OS (URLSession / WebKit);
	    - the OS's own passkey cryptography (AuthenticationServices);
	    - AES and AES-GCM inside the Rust core, used ONLY within authentication
	      protocols: the CTAP2 PIN/UV auth protocol to a security key
	      (`vela-core/src/ctap/pin_uv.rs`) and the encrypted tunnel of the
	      "sign in with your phone" passkey ceremony (caBLE / hybrid,
	      `vela-core/src/cable/`). Both protect the authentication exchange
	      itself — no user data is encrypted for storage or for confidentiality
	      in its own right.

	  US EAR Category 5 Part 2, Technical Note 1 to 5A002.a, excludes
	  cryptography limited to authentication (and the key management behind
	  it); OS-provided encryption is exempt. So the answer is NO non-exempt
	  encryption. It is the owner's declaration to sign — get counsel's view
	  before the production release. Without this key App Store Connect asks on
	  every upload, and a question answered by hand every time is a question
	  answered wrong once.
	-->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Vela uses the camera to scan QR codes — a payment request, or somebody's address.</string>
	<key>NSPhotoLibraryAddUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Vela saves your receive QR code to your album so you can share it.</string>
	<!--
	  One use: caBLE "sign in with your phone" (spec 019) — the BLE SCAN that
	  finds the phone or tablet that scanned our QR code, and the CTAP 2.3
	  L2CAP channel to it (`HybridCableScanner`, a `CBCentralManager`).

	  The Trusted Signer's nearby channel — this phone advertising as a GATT
	  peripheral (spec 075 T041) — was cut on 2026-09-23 (0801564b7), so the
	  sentence no longer mentions a computer in the room (spec 088): a purpose
	  string must say what the app does, and nothing it does not.
	-->
	<key>NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Vela uses Bluetooth to reach the nearby phone or tablet you sign in with — the one that scans the code on screen.</string>
	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>fetch</string>
	</array>
	<key>BGTaskSchedulerPermittedIdentifiers</key>
	<array>
		<string>app.getvela.VelaWallet.tracker</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
